---
title: "Design Chat System"
section: practice
level: advanced
duration: 60 min
description: "Design a real-time chat system with durable messaging, per-conversation ordering, multi-device delivery, presence, group fanout, and failure recovery."
registryId: practice-chat-system
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is a chat system?

A **chat system** accepts a message once, stores it durably, orders it within one conversation, and makes it available to every intended recipient device. A live WebSocket makes delivery feel immediate, but the durable conversation log is what lets the system recover after a disconnect, retry, or server failure.

The central invariant is: **after the sender receives an accepted acknowledgement, retrying the same client message ID cannot create another logical message, and every reader observes one stable conversation sequence.** Presence, typing indicators, and push notifications improve the experience, but none of them may decide whether a durable message exists.
{% /section-card %}

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Commit before accepted" eyebrow="Durability" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Authorize, deduplicate, assign the conversation sequence, and commit the message plus its outbox event before telling the sender it was accepted.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Order one conversation" eyebrow="Correctness" icon="shield" tone="violet" %}
Give each conversation one logical sequencing owner. Global ordering across unrelated conversations is unnecessary and expensive.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Replay after failure" eyebrow="Recovery" icon="repeat" tone="green" %}
Clients reconnect with a durable cursor, fetch missing sequence ranges, and apply messages idempotently before resuming the live stream.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% callout variant="info" %}
Attempt the design challenge before opening the walkthrough. Define what an acknowledgement means, draw the reconnect path, and state which features may degrade without blocking message acceptance.
{% /callout %}

{% design-challenge
   challengeId="chat-system"
   title="Design the durable message path"
   prompt="Design 1:1 and group chat for 100 million daily users and 50 million peak connections. Show where a client message ID is deduplicated, where conversation order is assigned, when the sender is acknowledged, how online devices receive the event, and how offline devices catch up." /%}

{% accordion defaultOpen="clarifying" %}

{% accordion-item id="clarifying" title="1. Clarify requirements and scope" %}

### What are chat-system requirements?

Requirements define the user promise before infrastructure choices begin. For this interview, a **message** is a durable logical record; a WebSocket frame, push notification, and per-device delivery are separate events.

### Functional requirements

- Send and receive text messages in 1:1 conversations and groups of up to 256 members.
- Show ordered conversation history with cursor-based pagination.
- Support several devices per user and synchronize missed messages after reconnect.
- Expose accepted, delivered, and read states without creating one mutable row per recipient-message pair.
- Show approximate presence and typing indicators.
- Upload media out of band, then send a message containing an authorized media reference.

### Non-functional goals

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric label="Delivery latency" value="<100ms" detail="Target p95 for online users in one region" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric label="Availability" value="99.99%" detail="Target for durable send and history APIs" tone="green" /%}
{% metric label="Connections" value="50M" detail="Assumed peak concurrent sockets" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric label="Ordering" value="Per conversation" detail="No global message order" tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

- Once `accepted` is returned, the message survives a process or gateway restart.
- A retry with the same `(sender_id, client_message_id)` returns the original message ID and sequence.
- Messages are totally ordered inside one conversation; presence, typing, and read indicators may converge eventually.
- Authorization is checked when accepting a send and again when serving history.
- Overload may delay fanout or disable optional signals, but it must not acknowledge an uncommitted message.

### Questions to ask the interviewer

1. Does "delivered" mean one user device received the event or every registered device did? Assume at least one active device.
2. Must a user see the same order on every device? Assume yes, using the server sequence.
3. Are edits, deletes, reactions, search, calls, and end-to-end encryption required now? Treat them as extensions represented by ordered events.
4. Can a temporarily unavailable conversation owner reject or queue sends instead of accepting them out of order? Assume yes.
5. How long is hot history retained? Assume 30 days hot and one year archived.

### Scope boundary

The core design includes durable text messaging, media references, groups, multi-device sync, receipts, presence, and push fallback. Full-text search, voice/video, public channels, bots, legal retention workflows, and end-to-end encryption key management are discussed as extensions rather than hidden inside the first design.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="calculations" title="2. Estimate scale and constraints" %}

### What is a capacity estimate?

A capacity estimate converts product assumptions into rates the architecture must absorb. The values below are modeling inputs, not claims about a specific commercial service.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric label="Daily messages" value="1B" detail="Logical messages before recipient fanout" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric label="Peak ingress" value="~58K/s" detail="Five times the daily average" tone="green" /%}
{% metric label="Delivery work" value="~232K/s" detail="Four recipients per message on average" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric label="Socket gateways" value="~2,858" detail="25K sockets each at 70% utilization" tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Back-of-the-envelope math

- **Average ingress:** `1,000,000,000 / 86,400 = 11,574 messages/s`.
- **Peak ingress:** `11,574 x 5 = 57,870 messages/s`.
- **Logical delivery attempts:** `57,870 x 4 recipients = 231,480 attempts/s` before multi-device expansion.
- **Raw message records:** `1B x 500 bytes = 500GB/day`; 30 hot days with replication factor 3 is about `45TB` before indexes and storage overhead.
- **Connection fleet:** `50M / 25K = 2,000` gateways at full utilization; dividing by a 70% target yields about `2,858` gateways before regional and failure headroom.
- **Media path:** if 5% of messages reference a 2MB object, object ingestion is about `100TB/day`. Media therefore bypasses the message log and uses object storage plus a CDN.

Fanout is the variable that averages hide. A 1:1 message has one recipient, while a busy group creates many delivery attempts. Batching recipients by destination gateway reduces queue and network overhead, but it does not reduce the final number of socket writes.

{% interactive-block id="practice/chat-system-capacity-lab" /%}

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Do not multiply every number into a precise fleet cost during an interview. State the assumed payload, peak factor, utilization target, and per-worker benchmark, then identify which estimate must be load-tested.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="3. Draw the high-level architecture" %}

### What is the high-level chat architecture?

The architecture separates connection ownership from message ownership. A nearby gateway owns a live socket; a conversation partition owns authorization, deduplication, and sequence assignment; asynchronous workers own delivery.

{% system-flow title="Durable chat message path" caption="Only the conversation commit is on the accepted path. Online fanout, push, receipts, and analytics happen after the durable boundary." %}
{% system-node title="Connection edge" eyebrow="Authenticate" icon="users" tone="blue" %}
Terminate WebSockets, enforce frame and connection limits, attach user/device identity, and forward commands. Gateways keep no authoritative history.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Conversation owner" eyebrow="Order" icon="route" tone="cyan" %}
Check membership, deduplicate the client message ID, and assign the next sequence under one fenced owner for the conversation.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Message log + outbox" eyebrow="Commit" icon="database" tone="violet" %}
Atomically persist the ordered message and an event for fanout. Return `accepted` only after this boundary succeeds.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Delivery plane" eyebrow="Distribute" icon="activity" tone="green" %}
Batch online recipients by gateway, update durable device cursors, retain offline history, and request best-effort mobile push.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Send path

1. The client creates a stable `client_message_id` before its first attempt.
2. The edge authenticates the connection and applies user, device, and payload limits.
3. The command routes by `conversation_id` to the conversation's home partition.
4. The owner verifies current membership and checks the sender's deduplication key.
5. The owner allocates the next sequence and commits the message plus outbox event.
6. The sender receives the stable `message_id`, sequence, and `accepted` state.
7. Fanout workers resolve recipient devices, batch live delivery by gateway, and leave offline devices to replay from history.

### Receive and reconnect path

1. A gateway pushes an envelope containing `conversation_id`, sequence, and message payload to a connected device.
2. The device persists and deduplicates the event locally, then advances its contiguous cursor.
3. After reconnect, the device requests `messages after sequence N` for each active conversation.
4. History fills any gap before the client treats newer live events as contiguous.
5. Delivery and read watermarks advance monotonically; duplicate acknowledgements are harmless.

### Regional ownership

Terminate sockets in the nearest healthy region, but route writes for one conversation to one home region at a time. A fenced lease prevents the old and new owners from assigning competing sequences during failover. Remote replicas serve disaster recovery and nearby history reads only when their freshness is acceptable.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="data" title="4. Design data, storage, and contracts" %}

### What does the data model protect?

The data model encodes the two correctness boundaries: one stable sequence per conversation and one logical message per sender-generated idempotency key. Ephemeral connection state belongs elsewhere.

{% concept-grid columns="2" %}
{% concept-card title="Conversation log" eyebrow="Source of truth" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Store immutable message events by `(conversation_id, bucket, sequence)`. A time or sequence bucket prevents one long-lived conversation from creating an unbounded physical partition.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Membership" eyebrow="Authorization" icon="shield" tone="violet" %}
Store member role, join sequence, leave sequence, and membership version. A fanout event carries the version used when the send was authorized.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Device cursor" eyebrow="Synchronization" icon="repeat" tone="green" %}
Track the highest contiguous delivered sequence per `(conversation, user, device)` so reconnect fetches a range instead of scanning all history.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Presence lease" eyebrow="Soft state" icon="activity" tone="amber" %}
Map a user-device to gateway and lease expiry. Rebuild it from live connections; never use it as proof that delivery succeeded.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Minimal records

- **Message:** `conversation_id`, bucket, sequence, `message_id`, `sender_id`, `client_message_id`, kind, encrypted-or-plain payload reference, membership version, and server timestamp.
- **Conversation:** type, home partition, current membership version, latest sequence, and retention policy.
- **Membership event:** user, role, effective sequence, action, and actor.
- **Device cursor:** device, highest contiguous delivered sequence, highest read sequence, and update time.
- **Outbox event:** message ID, conversation sequence, recipient snapshot reference, and publication state.

### Message contracts

- `send_message` includes protocol version, conversation ID, client message ID, content type, payload or media reference, and the last sequence observed by the sender.
- `message_accepted` returns the stable server message ID and assigned sequence. It does not claim recipient delivery.
- `message_event` is replayable and safe for a device to apply more than once.
- `sync_request` sends a conversation cursor and bounded page size; `sync_response` returns a contiguous range plus the next cursor.
- `receipt_update` advances delivered or read watermarks monotonically and ignores regressions.

The executable example demonstrates commit-before-ack and duplicate retry with a stable client message ID.

{% code-block file="/api/content/practice/chat-system/code/idempotent-message-ingest.js" language="javascript" title="Idempotent message acceptance" /%}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="deepdive" title="5. Deep-dive on delivery, presence, and fanout" %}

### What are delivery semantics?

Delivery semantics define what may happen when a client or worker retries after an uncertain result. The practical contract is **at-least-once transport with idempotent processing**, producing one visible logical message.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Accepted" label="Server durability" %}
The ordered message and outbox event are committed. The sender may stop retrying this client message ID.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Delivered" label="Device watermark" %}
At least one intended device confirms a contiguous sequence at or beyond this message.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Read" label="User action" %}
A client reports the highest sequence the user viewed. The server advances, never decreases, the watermark.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Synced" label="Recovery" %}
Every active device eventually fetches missing ranges; long-retired devices expire under policy.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Preserve order without global coordination

- Route one conversation to one fenced sequencing owner at a time.
- Assign a sequence only after authorization and deduplication checks.
- Let unrelated conversations progress independently.
- Allow sequence gaps if a reserved write aborts, but never reuse a committed sequence.
- Buffer a short out-of-order window on the client and fetch a missing range when the gap persists.

### Fan out by destination, not only by member

- Snapshot the authorized membership version for the message event.
- Expand recipients asynchronously so sender latency does not grow with group size.
- Group online devices by gateway and send one internal batch per destination gateway.
- Store one durable conversation message rather than one full payload copy per recipient.
- Advance recipient/device cursors in batches; use push only as a wake-up hint for offline mobile devices.

### Treat presence as a hint

Presence is a short lease refreshed by the active connection. Lease expiry, gateway failure, or network delay can make it stale. A false "online" result may waste a live-delivery attempt, while a false "offline" result may trigger an unnecessary push; neither result may lose the durable message.

Typing indicators are even weaker: throttle them, scope them to active conversation viewers, expire them quickly, and drop them first during overload.

Use the failure lab to test whether a recovery action protects durability, ordering, and one visible copy.

{% interactive-block id="practice/chat-system-delivery-failure-lab" /%}

{% callout variant="info" %}
[RFC 6455](https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc6455/) defines WebSocket's two-way framed transport, not chat acknowledgements or offline recovery. [Firebase Cloud Messaging delivery guidance](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/understand-delivery) likewise separates provider acceptance and reporting from the application's own end-to-end delivery state. Keep the chat contract above those transports.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="reliability" title="6. Plan scalability, reliability, and operations" %}

### What makes the system reliable?

Reliability means preserving the accepted-message invariant while dependencies fail and load becomes uneven. Recovery behavior must be designed before autoscaling rules.

{% concept-grid columns="2" %}
{% concept-card title="Gateway failure" eyebrow="Reconnect" icon="repeat" tone="blue" %}
Clients reconnect with jitter to a healthy gateway and replay after their durable cursors. The failed process owns sockets, not messages.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Conversation-owner failure" eyebrow="Fence" icon="shield" tone="rose" %}
Pause or queue sends until a new owner holds a higher fencing token. Never let two regions assign the same sequence range.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Fanout backlog" eyebrow="Backpressure" icon="activity" tone="amber" %}
Keep accepted messages durable, prioritize live text over optional signals, batch by gateway, and expose delivery delay instead of dropping work silently.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Presence or push outage" eyebrow="Degrade" icon="warning" tone="green" %}
Continue accepting and storing messages. Rebuild presence from sockets and let clients recover history without depending on push.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Scale each plane independently

- **Connection plane:** partition users across gateways, cap connections and outbound bytes per process, and drain nodes by asking clients to reconnect.
- **Command plane:** partition by conversation ID so authorization, deduplication, and sequence assignment share one owner.
- **Storage plane:** bucket large conversation logs, replicate durable records across failure domains, and archive old immutable segments.
- **Delivery plane:** partition outbox consumption by conversation, batch destinations by gateway, and bound retries with dead-letter inspection rather than silent loss.
- **Presence plane:** shard leases by user ID and rebuild them from gateway registrations.

### Overload order

1. Drop typing indicators and reduce presence refresh frequency.
2. Disable nonessential analytics and read-receipt fanout.
3. Batch gateway deliveries more aggressively and delay mobile push.
4. Apply per-sender and per-conversation admission control.
5. Reject new sends with a retryable error before the durable tier crosses its safety limit.

### Operate user-visible SLOs

- Accepted-message latency and error rate, split before and after durable commit.
- Online delivery p50, p95, and p99 from message commit to device acknowledgement.
- Reconnect success rate and missing-range replay duration.
- Oldest outbox age by conversation tier and destination region.
- Deduplication hits, duplicate client applies, and sequence-gap repairs.
- Presence staleness and push request failures as separate, noncritical indicators.
- Conversation-owner failovers, fencing rejections, and replica lag.

Test the recovery contract with lost acknowledgements, worker crashes after send but before checkpoint, expired presence leases, regional isolation, and a reconnect surge. A load test that covers only the healthy send path is incomplete.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="tradeoffs" title="7. Compare trade-offs and alternatives" %}

### What trade-offs define this design?

A strong answer names the rejected alternative and the consequence, not only the chosen technology.

{% concept-grid columns="2" %}
{% concept-card title="Commit before ack vs early ack" eyebrow="Durability" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Commit-before-ack adds storage latency but makes `accepted` meaningful. Early acknowledgement is faster only by shifting message-loss risk to the user.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Home region vs multi-writer" eyebrow="Ordering" icon="route" tone="violet" %}
A conversation home simplifies total order and deduplication. Multi-writer regions reduce local write latency but require conflict semantics users can understand.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Push fanout vs pull on sync" eyebrow="Delivery" icon="activity" tone="green" %}
Push gives low online latency; pull makes offline recovery simple. The hybrid design pushes to live gateways and always retains cursor-based history.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Receipt rows vs watermarks" eyebrow="Storage" icon="repeat" tone="amber" %}
Per-message receipt rows answer detailed audits but explode writes. Monotonic per-device watermarks compress the common "read through sequence N" case.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Additional decisions

- **Sequence counter vs broker offset:** a conversation-local counter keeps the API contract independent of broker topology; using a broker offset can simplify one implementation but couples repartitioning to user-visible order.
- **One payload vs recipient copies:** one immutable message plus cursors minimizes storage; recipient copies can simplify mailbox reads but amplify writes and deletion work.
- **Server-readable content vs end-to-end encryption:** server-readable content enables central search and moderation; E2EE moves trust and key recovery to clients and changes how abuse reporting, multi-device sync, and backups work. [Signal's protocol documentation](https://signal.org/docs/) is a primary reference for those protocol building blocks.
- **Exact presence vs scalable presence:** stronger consistency increases coordination but still cannot prove a remote device is reachable. Expiring soft state is the honest contract.

The selected design favors one conversation writer, durable commit before acknowledgement, asynchronous destination-batched fanout, and cursor-based replay. It spends a regional hop and some ownership complexity to obtain a crisp ordering and retry contract.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="wrapup" title="8. Wrap up with risks and extensions" %}

### Restate the design in one minute

1. Clients connect to nearby stateless gateways and attach stable user and device identities.
2. Sends route by conversation ID to one fenced owner that authorizes, deduplicates, sequences, and commits the message plus outbox event.
3. The sender receives `accepted` only after that commit.
4. Workers batch online recipients by gateway; offline devices later replay after their durable cursors.
5. Presence, typing, push, and analytics can degrade without changing message correctness.
6. Reconnect, deduplication, and range replay turn retries into one visible ordered conversation.

### Main risks

- **Hot conversations or abusive senders** can overload one ordering partition.
  - Isolate them with per-conversation quotas and dedicated partitions.
  - Measure queue age and owner CPU by conversation, not only fleet averages.
- **Split ownership during regional failover** can create conflicting sequences.
  - Require quorum-backed leases and monotonically increasing fencing tokens.
  - Prefer temporary unavailability to accepting ambiguous order.
- **Retry bugs** can create duplicate rows or duplicate visible messages.
  - Preserve the client ID through every hop and test crashes at each commit boundary.
  - Deduplicate both server acceptance and client application.
- **Membership races** can expose a message to a removed user or omit a new member.
  - Attach a membership version and define whether changes take effect at the assigned sequence.

### Extensions to discuss

- End-to-end encryption with device identity, prekeys, group key rotation, backup recovery, and explicit metadata leakage analysis.
- Message edits, deletes, reactions, and threads as ordered events referencing an original message.
- Very large public channels that replace per-member push fanout with partitioned subscription logs.
- Search indexes built asynchronously with deletion propagation and tenant-specific retention.
- Voice/video signaling over chat while media flows through a separate real-time communications plane.
- Enterprise tenancy, legal holds, audit export, and data residency by conversation or organization.

### Final interview checklist

- State exactly when a sender may stop retrying.
- Trace a lost acknowledgement without creating a duplicate logical message.
- Explain why presence and push are not delivery guarantees.
- Show how a reconnecting device detects and fills a sequence gap.
- Identify the partition key and the hot-key mitigation.
- Name the degraded mode for gateway, fanout, presence, storage, and regional failures.
- End with the first production signal: **oldest durable outbox age**, segmented by region and conversation tier.

{% /accordion-item %}

